  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:61",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine exhibits a consistent relationship with the insulin receptor through multiple indirect pathways, all of which involve the K-252a protein. The first pathway links benzocaine to the Tumor Susceptibility Gene 101 protein, which is targeted by 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, ultimately leading to the insulin receptor. The second pathway connects benzocaine to Solute Carrier Organic Anion Transporter Family Member 2A1, which is targeted by K-252a, and the third pathway links benzocaine to Glutamine-Fructose-6-Phosphate Aminotransferase [isomerizing], which is also targeted by K-252a. These pathways collectively suggest that benzocaine may influence insulin receptor function through the K-252a protein, which is a key mediator in the signaling cascade involving the insulin receptor.\n\nThe evidence supports a mechanistic link between benzocaine and the insulin receptor, mediated through K-252a, with the Tumor Susceptibility Gene 101 protein acting as a potential intermediate. The high scores in all three paths indicate a strong, albeit indirect, relationship, suggesting that benzocaine may modulate insulin receptor activity through a series of molecular interactions involving K-252a and its targets. The presence of multiple independent pathways strengthens the hypothesis, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the molecular network involved, emphasizing the need for further experimental validation to confirm the precise mechanism of action.",
